A||||10||Wrench||Fuel Pump Test ||||5||
Z||000000||Wrench||01-18-2000||09:54 PM||||Is there a "test point" or some connector I can put battery voltage on to test the fuel pump motor on an 86 V6 GT? I suspect my pump is going bad but want to be sure before I drop the tank. If the pump runs on this "test point" then my problem is elsewhere.  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||01-18-2000||10:25 PM||||There is a port to hook up a guage on the regulator. That would tell you more than just running the pump||||reg||1
Z||000002||cobra427||01-18-2000||11:24 PM||||Tom, I respectfully disagree with your placement of the "test valve".It is the fuel block that the "fuel pressure connection asm" is connected to and not the pressure regulator. At any rate,Wrench,get a fuel pressure guage and attach it to the fitting on the fuel block. It should have a cap on it.It will look like a tire valve stem underneth the cap.Use a rag to wrap around that area because it will leak a little gas during the connection. Start your engine and see if you don't have between 40.5-47 psi. at idle. If you do and it maintains that pressure at idle then your pump is ok. If not or it fails to hold the pressure then we'll have to go to plan "B". (Taken from the factory service manual,1986)||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||01-19-2000||10:43 AM||||Thanks cobra you are right. I was going from memory. The block is in the same area as the regulator. I used an a/c guage since the fitting is the same as a r12 fitting also as an AN3||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wrench||01-19-2000||07:43 PM||||What I was looking for was and "electrical test point" to run the fuel pump independent of it's normal control. I just wanted to be sure the pump motor would run. In any event I put a gauge on the schrader valve and barely had 10 psi of pressure!! Pump is coming out of the car. Guess after 14 years of use and 140,000 miles it finially got a little weak! Should be back up and running tomorrow. Thanks for the help!! Appreciate it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||01-20-2000||09:18 AM||||There is a 3 pin connector near where the two big wire bundles pass through from the engine compartment to the cabin.  It will have a black, brown, and lavender wire.  Unfortunately, I dont remember which wire is for the gauge, and which wire runs the pump.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ka4nkf||01-21-2000||07:59 PM||||You can short out the wires on the oil pressure sender unit and make it run.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||01-22-2000||11:26 AM||||How did you guys get your fuel pressure gauge on the shradder valve? I tried to get mine on and couldn't because the valve is to close to the intake manifold. Stupid GM engineers.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wrench||01-22-2000||03:04 PM||||I just took the little rubber hose that goes to the intake plenum loose and the gauge fitting screwed right on. It is a tight fit and you have to be sure to keep the gauge fitting straight. It also helps if you hold the gauge in one hand and pull down on the gauge fitting with the other as you screw it on. I put the hose back on after the gauge was installed. I only had 10 psi of pressure. Talk about poor performance.||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||01-22-2000||04:10 PM||||You have to have the car running with the gauge hooked up to get an accurate reading, you can't just hook up a gauge and turn the key to see 40 psi, you may only see 10psi if the car is not running.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wrench||01-23-2000||09:39 AM||||My understanding is the pump runs for a few seconds to build up pressure and you should see 45+ psi without the engine running. Think about it, the pump is electric and "mindlessly" goes about its job. If the engine is not started in a few seconds the pump shuts down, but the pressure must come up or she wont start. I was amazed what the new pump did!! Now I can hook my NO2 back up!! ||||reg||1
